NOTICE
======

This repository installs OpenWrt onto a Xiaomi AX3000T (RD03v2) by exploiting an
unpatched vulnerability in the stock firmware. Read this before running anything.

1. Authorised use only
----------------------
Use this only on devices you own. `chain.py` obtains root on the target without
credentials, and the vulnerability it uses is firmware-global: the units you can
reach this way are not necessarily yours. Unauthorised access to a computer
system is a criminal offence in most jurisdictions.

2. What this does to the device
-------------------------------
It writes to NAND. Specifically it roots the stock firmware, pivots into an
OpenWrt RAM initramfs, and runs `sysupgrade` to install the squashfs to flash,
having first set a root password and configured Wi-Fi for you. This **replaces
the vendor firmware**. It also consumes the one-shot `cap_init` trigger in the
process, which is what the exploit requires and is not reversible except by a
factory reset.

3. This install is effectively one-way
--------------------------------------
Stock keeps an A/B slot pair; the OpenWrt layout claims that region, so **the
vendor's fallback is gone once you install**. Getting back to stock is possible
and supported by this repo (`revert.py`, `restore.py`), and it works — but it
runs the same pivot again over the network, and if that fails your remaining
options are U-Boot TFTP recovery with a vendor-signed image, or a serial adapter.
There is no vendor support path for a device with third-party firmware.

4. Flashing can brick the device
--------------------------------
Writing firmware is inherently risky. A power loss, a bad image, or an
interrupted NAND write can leave the unit unusable without UART or TFTP
recovery. Vendor anti-rollback will refuse images older than the version the
unit last ran, so using a mismatched stock image for a revert will fail rather
than downgrade.

